From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 22:35:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: rndis: report the OEM's MaxTransferSize (1600)

With the CDC Ethernet descriptor (0021) and the open TX filter (0022) the TP26
BIOS brings up its side of the Redfish Host Interface -- it runs RNDIS_MSG_INIT
during POST and self-assigns 169.254.0.18 -- but the link is one-way.  Probing
from the BMC throughout POST gives:

    neigh 169.254.0.18 FAILED    pingreply=0     (52 samples)

We receive its ARPs; it never answers ours and never sends a single IP packet.
The BIOS never completes RNDIS negotiation: it does RESET/INIT/HALT four times,
never queries an OID and never sets OID_GEN_CURRENT_PACKET_FILTER, so its own
receive path stays shut and it discards our frames.

Disassembling the OEM gadget's INITIALIZE_CMPLT construction (eth.ko,
HandleRndisRequest) and comparing field by field, every value matches ours
except MaxTransferSize: the OEM writes 0x640 (1600), Linux computes
mtu + ethhdr + rndis_packet_msg_type + 22 = 1580 at the default MTU.

Widen the constant so the reply matches the implementation this BIOS accepts.
Kept MTU-relative rather than hardcoded.

TESTED ON HARDWARE 2026-08-11: this alone did NOT fix the link -- the RNDIS
trace was unchanged, the BIOS still did RESET/INIT/HALT x4 and never queried an
OID.  The actual defects were in the gadget state machine and are fixed by patch
0024.  This patch is retained only because 1600 is what the OEM reports and
parity with the implementation this BIOS is known to accept is worth having; it
is NOT load-bearing, and nothing depends on it.  Drop it freely if it ever gets
in the way.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/rndis.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/rndis.c
@@ -575,11 +575,22 @@
 	resp->DeviceFlags = cpu_to_le32(RNDIS_DF_CONNECTIONLESS);
 	resp->Medium = cpu_to_le32(RNDIS_MEDIUM_802_3);
 	resp->MaxPacketsPerTransfer = cpu_to_le32(1);
+	/*
+	 * Report the same MaxTransferSize as the AMI/MegaRAC gadget that the
+	 * TP26 BIOS accepts: 0x640 (1600).  Upstream's trailing +22 yields 1580
+	 * at the default MTU, and 1580 is the only field in INITIALIZE_CMPLT
+	 * that differs from the OEM's -- MajorVersion, MinorVersion,
+	 * DeviceFlags, Medium, MaxPacketsPerTransfer, PacketAlignmentFactor and
+	 * the AFList fields all match byte for byte.  The BIOS INIT/HALT-loops
+	 * against our reply and never proceeds to query any OID, so widen this
+	 * to match the implementation it does accept.  Kept MTU-relative rather
+	 * than hardcoded so a non-default MTU still scales.
+	 */
 	resp->MaxTransferSize = cpu_to_le32(
 		  params->dev->mtu
 		+ sizeof(struct ethhdr)
 		+ sizeof(struct rndis_packet_msg_type)
-		+ 22);
+		+ 42);
 	resp->PacketAlignmentFactor = cpu_to_le32(0);
 	resp->AFListOffset = cpu_to_le32(0);
 	resp->AFListSize = cpu_to_le32(0);
